# =============================================================================
# test-rig-sim.hal - the test rig's HAL with the board replaced by simulation
# =============================================================================
#
#   *** SIMULATOR ONLY. NEVER LOAD THIS ON THE MACHINE. ***
#
# Loaded in place of configs/test-rig/test-rig.hal by the INI that
# sim/make_sim_ini.py derives from configs/test-rig/test-rig.ini (it changes
# [HAL]HALFILE and [DISPLAY]DISPLAY and adds [SIM]; every other key is the
# rig's own). What is kept from test-rig.hal, and what replaces the board:
#
#   kept        trivkins, motmod at [EMCMOT]SERVO_PERIOD, the estop_latch chain,
#               the tool-change loopback, the servo-thread function order
#   replaced    hm2_eth stepgens  ->  motor-pos-cmd looped to motor-pos-fb
#               (no steps, so SCALE and step timing are NOT exercised)
#               hm2_eth link pins ->  sim/hm2_link_stub.py, same names
#               the five switches ->  sim_home_switch, wired as PLAN-BUGUN
#                                     Faz 5 plans them (X corrected)
#
# Switch positions are INVENTED for the simulator and say nothing about the rig,
# whose travel is unmeasured (PLAN-BUGUN Faz 4). They are in motor units, and
# every motor starts at 0 when LinuxCNC starts, i.e. between its switches, on
# the side homing searches toward.
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
# LOADS
# =============================================================================

loadrt [KINS]KINEMATICS
loadrt [EMCMOT]EMCMOT servo_period_nsec=[EMCMOT]SERVO_PERIOD num_joints=[KINS]JOINTS
loadrt estop_latch

# One simulated switch per physical switch on the rig (docs/DURUM.md:69-80).
# sim_home_switch: a negative home_pos is a switch on the negative side, which
# reads TRUE at or below it; a positive one on the positive side
# (reference/linuxcnc/src/hal/components/sim_home_switch.comp).
loadrt sim_home_switch names=sim-x-min,sim-x-max,sim-y-min,sim-y-max,sim-z-top

# Stand-in for the board's link-health pins (see the script's docstring).
loadusr -Wn hm2_7i92.0 python3 [SIM]LINK_STUB


# =============================================================================
# THREADS
# =============================================================================
# Same order as test-rig.hal, with the switches where hm2 read was (inputs
# first) and nothing where hm2 write was.

addf sim-x-min                         servo-thread
addf sim-x-max                         servo-thread
addf sim-y-min                         servo-thread
addf sim-y-max                         servo-thread
addf sim-z-top                         servo-thread
addf motion-command-handler            servo-thread
addf motion-controller                 servo-thread
addf estop-latch.0                     servo-thread


# =============================================================================
# JOINTS - position loopback in place of the stepgens
# =============================================================================
# The emcmot.0N.enable signals are kept so halcmd shows the same names as on
# the rig; nothing reads them here.

newsig emcmot.00.enable bit
net emcmot.00.enable <= joint.0.amp-enable-out
net motor.00.pos joint.0.motor-pos-cmd => joint.0.motor-pos-fb sim-x-min.cur-pos sim-x-max.cur-pos

newsig emcmot.01.enable bit
net emcmot.01.enable <= joint.1.amp-enable-out
net motor.01.pos joint.1.motor-pos-cmd => joint.1.motor-pos-fb sim-y-min.cur-pos sim-y-max.cur-pos

newsig emcmot.02.enable bit
net emcmot.02.enable <= joint.2.amp-enable-out
net motor.02.pos joint.2.motor-pos-cmd => joint.2.motor-pos-fb sim-z-top.cur-pos


# =============================================================================
# SWITCHES - as PLAN-BUGUN Faz 5 plans them, not as test-rig.hal has them
# =============================================================================
# On the rig these nets are still commented out, and homing there would search
# forever. Here they are connected, so homing can be exercised. A simulated
# switch reads TRUE when pressed, like `.in_not` on the rig.
#
# X: gpio.021 (left, minimum) is home + negative limit, gpio.020 (right,
#    maximum) the positive limit - the corrected mapping, PLAN-BUGUN Faz 5.
# Y: which of gpio.022 / gpio.023 is the minimum is UNKNOWN (Y DIR fault).
#    The simulator assumes Y is wired like X.
# Z: gpio.024, top only: home + positive limit. No bottom switch.

setp sim-x-min.home-pos -10.0
setp sim-x-max.home-pos 145.0
setp sim-y-min.home-pos -10.0
setp sim-y-max.home-pos 145.0
setp sim-z-top.home-pos   5.0

net x-home-limit sim-x-min.home-sw => joint.0.home-sw-in joint.0.neg-lim-sw-in
net x-max-limit  sim-x-max.home-sw => joint.0.pos-lim-sw-in
net y-home-limit sim-y-min.home-sw => joint.1.home-sw-in joint.1.neg-lim-sw-in
net y-max-limit  sim-y-max.home-sw => joint.1.pos-lim-sw-in
net z-home-limit sim-z-top.home-sw => joint.2.home-sw-in joint.2.pos-lim-sw-in


# =============================================================================
# ESTOP CHAIN - identical to test-rig.hal: GUI only, watchdog as fault source
# =============================================================================
# Kept identical on purpose: after a software estop the rig stays in
# ESTOP_RESET, because iocontrol.0.user-enable-out is not in this chain, and
# the UI's reset logic is written for that.

net user-enable   iocontrol.0.user-request-enable => estop-latch.0.reset
net enable-latch  estop-latch.0.ok-out            => iocontrol.0.emc-enable-in
net watchdog      hm2_7i92.0.watchdog.has_bit     => estop-latch.0.fault-in


# =============================================================================
# TOOL CHANGE LOOPBACK - identical to test-rig.hal
# =============================================================================
net tool-prep-loop   iocontrol.0.tool-prepare => iocontrol.0.tool-prepared
net tool-change-loop iocontrol.0.tool-change  => iocontrol.0.tool-changed
